  <h2>How We Research and Test</h2>

  <p>EasyHTPC publishes two types of content. We are transparent about which is which.</p>

  <h3>Hands-On Reviews</h3>
  <p>For products Thomas has personally used &mdash; hardware in his server rack, software in his Docker stack, remotes and input devices tested against real HTPC setups &mdash; reviews are written from direct experience. You will find notes on real configuration issues, performance under actual load, and things that spec sheets do not tell you.</p>

  <h3>Research-Based Buying Guides</h3>
  <p>For broader category roundups covering products Thomas has not personally owned, we draw on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Manufacturer specifications and official documentation</li>
    <li>Community consensus from r/htpc, r/PleX, r/DataHoarder, and specialist forums</li>
    <li>Published benchmarks from trusted hardware reviewers</li>
    <li>30+ years of hardware expertise applied to evaluating what the numbers actually mean for real-world use</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Research-based guides are framed as buying guides, not personal reviews. We note this distinction within each article.</p>

  <hr>

  <h2>Editorial Independence</h2>

  <p>EasyHTPC participates in affiliate programs including the Amazon Associates Program. When you purchase through a link on this site, we may earn a commission at no additional cost to you.</p>

  <p><strong>Affiliate relationships do not influence our recommendations.</strong> A product that does not earn a commission but genuinely serves our readers will be recommended over one that does. Products we consider overpriced, poorly built, or poor value are not recommended regardless of affiliate availability. We have turned down sponsored content and paid placements.</p>

  <p>Our test for any recommendation: <em>Would Thomas put this in his own setup?</em> If the answer is no, it does not make the list.</p>

